-v5.1.0-rollback-sev2", "confidence": 0.9}, "name": "create_incident", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}, {"id": "toolu_014bEBGd29A8QvU8r4urAHcg", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-api", "confidence": 0.9}, "name": "get_service_status", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I rolled payments back to its last healthy release and opened the SEV2 incident. Errors are back to normal.\n\n- **Affected service:** The service is called `payments-api` (there's no service named plain \"payments\"). It was running v5.1.0 in all three regions (us-east, us-west, eu-west), marked unhealthy with an 18% error rate. Its release history has v5.1.0 as degraded and v5.0.0 as the most recent release that was healthy in production.\n- **Rollback:** `payments-api` is now on v5.0.0 in all three regions. A follow-up check shows it healthy, with the error rate down from 18% to 0.2%. This didn't need change-manager approval, because v5.0.0 has been healthy in production before.\n- **Incident:** I opened INC-0001 as a SEV2 for `payments-api`, and on-call has been paged. It describes the bad v5.1.0 deploy and the rollback, and lists next steps: keep watching the recovery and find out what in v5.1.0 caused the errors.\n\nI only checked a few likely service names for payments. If there are other payments services under different names that were part of this morning's deploy, I haven't looked at them.", "type": "text"}]}]}